<package>
  <metadata>
    <id>recur</id>
    <version>0.2.8</version>
    <title>Recur</title>
    <authors>User Level Up Contributors</authors>
    <owners>userlevelup</owners>
    <summary>Trace hierarchy deltas across code, docs, and workflow state</summary>
    <description><![CDATA[**recur** helps developers find deltas in hierarchical structures without standing up a database. The 0.2.5 package line made meaningful file names practical for comparing related work; 0.2.6 clarified folders as traceable hierarchy maps; 0.2.7 emphasized traceability across structure, state, and related work.

Version 0.2.8 carries that curve into a broader eventness workflow: reveal capsules for resuming context, psyche/status inspection, trace-id and trace-stats analysis, flatten/merge composition, pure `recur watch` queries over `recur-watch` state, pure `recur version` queries over artifact manifests written by `recur-version`, and self-reporting Warp bubble composition with confirmation-gated Slice completion through `recur-warp`.

At its core, **recur** is a command-line tool for working with hierarchically named code, docs, generated artifacts, and identifiers whose names encode structure using dot-separated or custom conventions.

While tools like `grep` and `ripgrep` excel at fast text matching, they treat results as flat lists. `recur` complements them by understanding hierarchy, so you can search, navigate, compare, and analyze related work as a structured system.

## Features
- Hierarchy-aware pattern matching with `*` and `**` wildcards
- Multi-separator merge - unify results across naming conventions (`.` and `_`)
- Project-aware config via `.recur/config.toml` (`recur init`, `recur init --analyze`)
- Unix pipe composability - stdin mode for pure pipe workflows
- Scoped text search within a hierarchy (grep-like, but structure-aware)
- Tree visualization using Unicode box-drawing characters
- Reveal capsules for context recovery and lane-local handoff
- Watcher-state queries through pure `recur watch`
- Artifact version policy, manifest, and history queries through pure `recur version`
- Optional companion binaries for active workflow runners (`recur-git`, `recur-watch`, `recur-version`, `recur-warp`, when included in release zip)
- JSON output for tooling integration

## Commands
- `recur init` - initialize or analyze `.recur/config.toml`
- `recur reveal` - surface lane-local `*.recur.md` context capsules
- `recur psyche` - inspect project eventness/status documents
- `recur watch` - list, filter, and explain active watcher state
- `recur version` - inspect artifact version policy, manifests, and preserved history
- `recur warp map|merge|status` - inspect target Slice maps and composed completion coverage
- `recur files` - find files by hierarchical pattern
- `recur find` - search text within a hierarchy scope
- `recur tree` - visualize hierarchy as a tree
- `recur related` - find sibling files
- `recur children` - find child files
- `recur id` - search for hierarchical identifiers
- `recur stats` - analyze hierarchy statistics
- `recur callers` - find all places where a function/method is called
- `recur callees` - find all functions/methods a given function calls
- `recur trace` - multi-level call graph visualization
- `recur merge` - merge results across separators
- `recur flatten` - flatten structured files (XML, JSON) into hierarchical dot-paths
- `recur trait` - manage project trait config (`list`, `get`, `set` in `.recur/config.toml`)
- `recur trace-id` - trace a hierarchical identifier through files; classify as define/produce/consume/trigger
- `recur trace-stats` - call-graph metrics: direct, transitive, circular, depth, risk
- `recur-git checkpoint` - capture parallel-lane git/todo checkpoints
- `recur-watch` - run the active watch/subscription loop and write ACK/NAK status records
- `recur-version` - save artifact snapshots, update manifests, and write ACK/NAK status records
- `recur-warp complete` - dry-run or confirm one contract-bound accepted Slice completion layer

Named as a quiet tribute to Dennis M. Ritchie's early work on recursive program structure.
]]></description>
